CORE
main.cpp
--cpp20 --object-bits 11 --no-unwinding-assertions
^\[main\.assertion\.1\] line \d+ empty pack deduced: SUCCESS$
^\[main\.assertion\.2\] line \d+ pack of 1 deduced: SUCCESS$
^\[main\.assertion\.3\] line \d+ pack of 2 deduced: SUCCESS$
^\[main\.assertion\.4\] line \d+ pack of 3 deduced: SUCCESS$
^\[main\.assertion\.5\] line \d+ wrong value FAILs \(non-vacuous\): FAILURE$
^VERIFICATION FAILED$
^EXIT=10$
^SIGNAL=0$
--
^CONVERSION ERROR$
--
[temp.spec.partial.match] / [temp.class.spec] / [expr.sizeof]/5: a class
template partial specialization whose pattern is a template-id with a pack
expansion (primary<Types...>) must match a concrete instantiation, deducing the
pack for any length (including 0 and 1).  CBMC matched the nested template-id
arguments positionally and never deduced the pack (so the partial specialization
was never selected, and member accesses were silently dropped), and a
single-element pack's sizeof... was mis-evaluated.  This is the shape of
std::tuple_size<std::tuple<Types...>>.  Assertions 1-4 prove packs of 0/1/2/3 are
deduced; assertion 5 (a wrong value) is expected to FAIL, proving the values are
genuinely checked (non-vacuous).
